lesson writing-a-capital-and-a-full-stop v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=3 subject=writing levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A capital letter and a full stop"
  goal "Start a sentence with a capital letter and finish it with a mark."

  look {
    scene 35x17 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        row top space=1 align=centre {
          art pic asset="cat"
          column words space=1 align=start {
            sentence strip before="Kim has a cat" blank=0 after="."
            wordcards cards words=["kim", "has", "a", "cat"] picked=0
          }
        }
        row talk space=1 align=centre {
          guide g pose=point
          bubble b "A name keeps its capital wherever it sits." width=22 from=g.hand
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      Two words in a sentence nearly always need a capital letter. The first word needs one,
      because the sentence is starting. A name needs one, wherever it sits. On the cards, kim is
      still small. The mark at the end says the sentence has finished.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice writing.needs-a-capital count=1 seed=2
    level easy {
      worked writing.capital-count k=0 level=medium
    }
    practice writing.capital-count count=2 seed=3
    practice writing.capital-fix count=2 seed=5
    practice writing.where-it-ends count=2 hard-count=1 seed=4 hard-seed=7
    level hard {
      practice writing.robot-mistake-kind count=1 seed=1
    }
    show writing.which-mark k=2 hard-k=1
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show writing.robot-mistake-kind s=1 m=1 hard-m=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show writing.three-capitals k=0
  }
  remember {
    say "Capital at the start, capital for a name, mark at the end."
  }

  grown-ups """
    Children who can say the rule still forget it while writing, because the writing takes all of
    their attention. Ask them to check the two capitals after they finish rather than while they go.
    The counting questions give a sentence with no capitals at all, so the child has to find the
    start, the names, the days and I without being told which. In the two-star question the point is
    to say which kind of mistake the robot made; in the three-star one, let the child plan the
    sentence out loud before any of it is written.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the name to write is as written, a line with four capitals is counted once in the teacher's pen before lines with two, the capital to fix is the first word or a name, the full stop goes after a long first sentence, the mark is a full stop, and the two-star and three-star are as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the name to write is as written, the lines to count hold three or four capitals with a day of the week or I among them, the capital to fix is I or Monday, the full stop goes after red in the kite's two short sentences, one of them making way for a second robot mistake, the mark is an exclamation mark, the two-star's mistake is a missing full stop, and the three-star is as written."
  }
}
